.TH AENIGMA-STATUS 8 "2026-08-12" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-status \- display the running status of the Aenigma service
and its configured onion services
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-status
prints a human-readable, color-coded summary of the local Aenigma
installation: whether the
.B aenigma
and
.B tor
systemd services are running, and, for every Tor hidden service
configured under
.IR /var/lib/tor ,
its name, published
.I .onion
address, and configured port(s). It takes no options or arguments.
.PP
The script performs the following actions:
.IP \(bu 2
Reports the active/inactive state of the
.B aenigma
and
.B tor
systemd units via
.BR "systemctl is-active" .
.IP \(bu 2
Iterates every subdirectory of
.IR /var/lib/tor .
Any subdirectory without a
.I hostname
file is treated as a hidden service that Tor has not yet started or
finished configuring, and is silently skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
For each remaining hidden service, reads its published address from
.IR /var/lib/tor/ SERVICE / hostname
and looks up the matching
.B HiddenServicePort
entry or entries for that service by parsing
.I /etc/tor/torrc
directly.
.IP \(bu 2
Prints a block per service showing its name, address, and port(s),
or a note that no onion services are configured yet if none were
found.
.IP \(bu 2
Prints a closing hint pointing to
.BR aenigma-tor-get-auth (8)
for retrieving client authorization keys, where configured.
.PP
Output uses ANSI color escape sequences and is intended for
interactive terminal use.
.PP
.B aenigma-status
must be run as root, since
.I /var/lib/tor
and its
.I hostname
files are only readable by the
.B debian-tor
user and root.
.SH OPTIONS
.B aenigma-status
takes no command-line options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Status information was gathered and printed. This is returned even
if the
.B aenigma
or
.B tor
services are stopped, or no onion services are configured; those
are reported conditions, not script failures.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not run as root.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/tor
Scanned for hidden service subdirectories.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tor/ SERVICE /hostname
Read to obtain each onion service's published address.
.TP
.I /etc/tor/torrc
Parsed to determine the configured port(s) for each onion service.
Read-only; never modified by this script.
.SH EXAMPLES
Display current Aenigma and Tor status:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-status
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The
.I torrc
parser implemented in this script
.RB ( get_ports_for_service )
is a plain-text, line-by-line scan that assumes each
.B HiddenServiceDir
block is contiguous and terminated by either the next
.B HiddenServiceDir
line or end of file. It does not use Tor's own configuration parser,
so any
.I torrc
file with unusual formatting, comments interleaved inside a block,
or a
.B HiddenServiceDir
value that differs only by trailing whitespace or path
normalisation from the directory name under
.I /var/lib/tor
may be reported with the wrong port, or as
.IR "unknown port" .
.PP
A hidden service directory without a
.I hostname
file is silently omitted from the output entirely, with no
indication to the operator that a configured-but-not-yet-started
service exists. An operator troubleshooting a service that never
came up may see no mention of it at all in
.BR aenigma-status 's
output.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR tor (8),
.BR aenigma-start (8),
.BR aenigma-tor (8),
.BR aenigma-tor-get-auth (8),
.BR torrc (5)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.ruja@aenigma.ro>
